@meta
id: 25-09
title: Mixing addresses in one range
chapter: 25
type: lesson
skills: range-syntax, range-pattern
par: 36
minutes: 6
vscode: click above the method, scroll, shift-click its closing brace, Delete
open: customer.provider.ts

@brief
`legacyExport` has not been called since the reporting service moved off
JSON dumps. Delete the method and the blank line above it, so the file
reads as if it had never been there.

The two ends of a range do not have to be the same kind of address. Here
the top of what you want is one line above the cursor and the bottom is a
line you can only describe by searching. So: `.-1` on the left, a pattern
on the right.

The closing brace of a method sits at two spaces of indent. `^  }` picks
it out and will not match the class's own brace in column 1.

@keys
| `:.-1,/^  }/d` | from the line above the cursor to the next two-space `}` |
| `:.,/pat/y` | yank from here down to a match |
| `:?pat?,.d` | delete from a match above down to here |

@hints
Search to the `legacyExport` signature first. That line is `.`, and it is
the second line of what you want gone.
--
Left address: the line above the cursor. Right address: the method's own
closing brace, found by pattern.
--
`/legacyExport` Enter, then `:.-1,/^  }/d` Enter, then `:wq`.

@solution
    /legacyExport<Enter>  :.-1,/^  }/d<Enter>  :wq<Enter>

Thirty-one keystrokes. `dap` from chapter 10 would also have worked here,
and on a tidy method it is fewer keys. It stops working the moment the
body contains a blank line, because vim then sees two paragraphs.
`.-1,/^  }/` does not care: it is anchored to the structure of the code
rather than to the blank lines that happen to be in it.

Each address in a comma-separated range is worked out from the cursor's
position, independently. Both `.-1` and `/^  }/` are relative to the line
you searched to, not to each other.

The one exception is worth knowing. A semicolon instead of a comma moves
the cursor to the first address before the second is resolved:

    :/BEGIN/;/END/d       resolve END from BEGIN's line, not from here
    :.;+5d                identical to :.,+5d, since . is where you are

With two patterns, `;` is usually what you meant, and it is why
`:/BEGIN/,/END/` can be surprising when an `END` sits above the cursor
but below the `BEGIN`. `,` is right for the common case; reach for `;`
when the second address only makes sense from the first.

@start customer.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { CustomerRepository } from './customer.repository';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';

@injectable()
export class CustomerProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(CustomerRepository) protected customerRepository: CustomerRepository,
  ) {}

  public async findOne(id: string) {
    const customer = await this.customerRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!customer) {
      throw exception('CUSTOMER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return customer;
  }

  public async legacyExport(id: string) {
    const customer = await this.findOne(id);

    return JSON.stringify(customer, null, 2);
  }

  public async archive(id: string) {
    await this.findOne(id);

    return this.customerRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { archived: true },
    });
  }
}

@expected customer.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { CustomerRepository } from './customer.repository';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';

@injectable()
export class CustomerProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(CustomerRepository) protected customerRepository: CustomerRepository,
  ) {}

  public async findOne(id: string) {
    const customer = await this.customerRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!customer) {
      throw exception('CUSTOMER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return customer;
  }

  public async archive(id: string) {
    await this.findOne(id);

    return this.customerRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { archived: true },
    });
  }
}
